# SIGN-C — the grammar for a turn whose only output is a local decision.
#
# `cortex.gbnf` stays the general contract and is not touched: the slow
# clock's vocabulary is the 42 atoms in all four fields, because a cortex
# that can only name actions cannot think anything else. This file is not
# a smaller vocabulary. It is a different question.
#
# The first real 4B run failed on exactly that difference. Asked what the
# hazard sign meant, it answered:
#
#     AMOR → CAMBIAR → TEMER @AHORA
#
# which is a well-formed WSP and a fair reading of the sentence — the
# thing that changed is now to be feared. `parse_reply` accepted it, so it
# was not a reject; but `loop` only turns a **rel** of UNIR / TEMER /
# OBSERVAR into a proposal, and this line has TEMER in DST. So it produced
# no action, and the operator gate was red with `qwen_rejected = 0`: the
# model had read the sign and said so in a shape the body cannot take.
#
# The fix is not to teach the parser that sentence, and not to widen what
# counts as an action. It is to ask the question in the shape the answer
# has to have. This report scores one local proposal, so SRC, DST and TIME
# are fixed and the only free field is the relation:
#
#     YO → { UNIR | TEMER | OBSERVAR | NONE } → AQUI @AHORA
#
# UNIR is approach, TEMER is avoid, OBSERVAR is wait. NONE is the fourth
# on purpose: it parses as an atom and maps to no action, so abstaining is
# sayable. A grammar of three would make every turn produce a verb, and a
# cortex that cannot decline is a cortex that cannot be wrong.
#
# The shape is byte-for-byte the shape `cortex.gbnf` produces, so the same
# `parse_reply` reads both and nothing downstream knows which was used.
#
#   llama-cli --grammar-file echoai/nexus0/cortex_action.gbnf ...

root ::= src " " arrow " " rel " " arrow " " dst " @" time "\n"

arrow ::= "→"

src ::= "YO"
rel ::= "UNIR" | "TEMER" | "OBSERVAR" | "NONE"
dst ::= "AQUI"
time ::= "AHORA"
